{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/168915": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Applied Health Informatics (MS)",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences Location: Lincoln Center Campus Fordham University is seeking applications to teach graduate-level courses in the Master of Science in Applied Health Informatics. Supporting our aim in building a strong pool of instructors, we encourage highly motivated professionals to join our team in leading the curriculum of this growing program. On-site instruction will take place at our Lincoln Center Campus in Manhattan, NY. Courses are delivered in a hybrid format, combining online instruction with in-person sessions. The ideal candidate brings both academic knowledge and industry experience to guide students in applying principles to healthcare settings. Part-time faculty members will also supervise student projects and capstones. Openings are available starting with the fall 2025 Semester. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS: Teach 1-2 courses per term. Deliver instruction through a hybrid model (asynchronous or synchronous online with in-person meetings each term). Engage students in active learning, facilitate discussions, and encourage real-world application of course content. Assess student performance through assignments, exams, and projects; provide timely and constructive feedback. Maintain regular virtual office hours and respond to student inquiries in a timely manner. Collaborate with program leadership and instructional designers to ensure high-quality course delivery. Stay current with trends in health informatics and integrate relevant industry examples into instruction. Uphold academic integrity and foster a supportive learning environment . Master’s degree in Health Informatics, Biomedical Informatics, Health Information Management, Public Health, Healthcare Administration, Computer Science, Data Science or a related field from a college or university accredited by the US Department of Education or an international equivalent. Minimum 3 years of professional experience in a healthcare or health IT setting. Prior teaching experience at the graduate or professional level (preferably in a hybrid or online format). Proficiency with Learning Management Systems (Blackboard) and virtual meeting platforms (Zoom) will be helpful. Excellent communication and organizational skills. Please apply to the position by following the Interfolio link ( Apply Now )"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/132172": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Communication and Media Studies Dept",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Communication and Media Studies Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campus *This is an ongoing ad for part-time faculty within the department* The Department of Communication and Media Studies at Fordham University seek experienced, qualified instructors to undertake teaching and related duties across our undergradute and Masters programs: Communication and Culture Digital Technology and Emerging Media Film and Television Studies Journalism Masters in Public Media Teaching takes place in person at the university’s Rose Hill campus (Bronx) or Lincoln Center (Manhattan). In most cases a pre-existing syllabus is available for use by instructors.  In the majority of cases, classes meet twice a week for 75 minutes. Some evening classes and production classes meet once a week for 2.5 hours. In addition to this class time, instructors are required to hold regular weekly office hours (which can be held remotely). Class enrollment sizes typically range from 15-35 students. Part-Time faculty at Fordham University are represented by Fordham Faculty United , part of the SEIU Union. A collective bargaining agreement between FFU and Fordham University is in place and can be viewed here . Qualified applicants will have (as a minimum) a Masters in a cognate subject and will ideally hold a PhD (or be enrolled in a PhD program) in a relevant field. We also typically require that instructors join us with a minimum of two years relevant teaching experience in higher education. The Department of Communication and Media Studies at Fordham University has moved to using Interfolio to support its hiring requirements. Please note that as of September 2023, we only accept inquiries and applications to teach via this platform. If you are interested in teaching in our department, please use Interfolio to submit an application. The application process involves the submission of the following documents: Current C.V. List of courses currently / previously taught (including institution and semester/year) Syllabus (if you have previosuly designed a syllabus) Diversity Statement (max 1 page) Reference Contact (1) Course Choices* *During your aplication you will be invited to identify which of our courses you are qualified to teach. Please visit our course catalog and note down the course code and course title for each course you believe you would be able to teach. Please also include a short statement outlining your relevant experience / expertise to teach this course."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/130491": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Computer and Information Science",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Computer and Information Sciences Location: Rose Hill or Lincoln Center Campus The Department of Computer and Information Science at Fordham University offers undergraduate, master's, and doctoral programs in computer science and related fields in the Bronx and Manhattan campuses. For information about the department, visit our website . The department invites applications for multiple adjunct positions to start in Fall 2023. Incumbents are expected to teach up to 2 courses per semester (on-campus or non-online or in dual-modality). The course list includes: Undergraduate classes: C Programming, Data Structures, Operating Systems, Computer Networks, Databases, Cybersecurity, Forensic Computing Graduate classes: Machine Learning, Big Data Computing All graduate courses and some undergraduate courses are offered in the evening, and meet once a week. All classes are capped at thirty. This position is covered under the SEIU Local 200 union. For further information, please visit the following website: Information for Adjunct Faculty . The positions require a M.S. in Computer Science or related fields or B.S. with relevant industrial or teaching experience, a commitment to teaching excellence, and excellent communication skills. All applications should include a cover letter with qualifications, the curriculum vitae, and optional materials such as contact information for one to three references, previous teaching materials, and teaching evaluations."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193408": {
      "title": "*Tenured: Professor, Bepler Chair in Natural and Applied Sciences, Department of Computer & Information Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Computer and Information Sciences Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Department of Computer and Information Science (CIS) at Fordham University invites applications for the Kim B. and Stephen E. Bepler Chair in the Natural and Applied Sciences. This is one of five Bepler Chairs at Fordham, made possible through generous endowments from Kim and Steve Bepler, to recognize scientists of exceptional scholarly impact who inspire interdisciplinary approaches that engage and excite the next generation of scientists. This newly endowed Chair is a focal point of the University’s strategy to provide thought leadership for Fordham's efforts to develop innovative technologies and intelligence that advance the common good. The successful candidate will be able to cultivate, build, and inspire institutional practices in research and teaching connected to AI applications across the University, the discipline, and broader industries. The Bepler Chair will facilitate inter- and trans-disciplinary research that advances solutions to some of society’s greatest scientific and technological challenges—and does so in ways that promote ethical practices that ultimately benefit individuals, communities, and the health of our planet. Ongoing research in the department includes generative AI, cybersecurity, quantum computing, robotics, computational finance, collaborations with biology and neuroscience, and partnerships with IBM, Columbia, NYU, Albert Einstein College of Medicine, and the Bronx Zoo/Botanical Gardens. The Bepler Chair in the Natural and Applied Sciences includes a tenured appointment in the CIS Department at an appropriate rank, and comes with a reduced teaching load and a research fund supported by the endowment. The Chair holder will be a distinguished researcher and a gifted teacher, and will promote integrated interdisciplinary approaches across the natural and applied sciences and related fields. Initial appointment to the Chair is for five years, with the possibility of renewal conditional on continuing scholarly contributions consistent with the terms of the Chair. The expected salary range at the rank of associate is $160,000 to $190,000, and for a full professor is $195,000 to $225,000. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Computer Science or related fields. Successful candidates will demonstrate strong research potential, including the ability to secure external funding, a commitment to excellence in teaching, and effective communication skills. All application materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio at this link: Bepler Chair Natural and Applied Sciences . Please review the position announcement carefully, including the required qualifications, application materials, and submission deadline. Applicants should upload all materials identified below. Cover letter or letter of interest Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement or teaching philosophy Writing samples or selected publications Contact References Please contact Dr. Gary Weiss (gaweiss@fordham.edu ) for inquiries. For fullest consideration, applications should be submitted by November 15th, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192576": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor, Inorganic Chemistry",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Chemistry and Biochemistry Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at Fordham University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Inorganic Chemistry beginning in Fall 2027 at the Rose Hill campus. Areas of specialization include experimental and theoretical approaches in inorganic chemistry, broadly defined, including coordination chemistry, bioinorganic chemistry, organometallic chemistry, catalysis, and solid-state/materials chemistry. The successful applicant is expected to establish a strong, externally funded research program that engages undergraduate researchers and leads to publications in peer-reviewed journals. Teaching responsibilities will include inorganic chemistry lecture and laboratory courses, with the possibility of teaching general chemistry or courses aligned with the candidate’s expertise. Applications received by September 30, 2026, will receive priority consideration and will be reviewed as they are received. Candidates should hold a Ph.D. in Chemistry and have postdoctoral research experience. Candidates should submit a cover letter, CV, unofficial transcripts, a research statement, a teaching statement, and contact information for at least three references to https://apply.interfolio.com/192576. Official graduate transcripts will be required of the finalist."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/135284": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Economics Department",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Economics Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Department of Economics at Fordham University periodically hires part-time non-tenure track (NTT) faculty to teach introductory courses in Economics and Statistics. Based on scheduling needs as well as the qualifications and availability of the applicant, successful candidates may be offered one or two sections of Principles of Microeconomics or Principles of Macroeconomics, or one or two sections of Statistics I or II. These are core courses in the Economics Major and the Gabelli School of Business core. We regularly hire part-time faculty for fall (mid-Aug to end Dec) and spring (mid January to end May). These positions are by semester positions. Part-time NTT instructors at Fordham are represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and certain terms of employment, including minimum per course rates and details concerning benefits, are set by the Adjunct Faculty Collective Bargaining Agreement . For further information, please visit the following website: Information for Adjunct Faculty . Our department serves a diverse student body at two campuses in New York City, at Lincoln Center in Manhattan and at the larger Rose Hill campus in the Bronx. In person at either the Rose Hill campus in the Bronx or the Lincoln Center campus in Manhattan (depending on teaching need) Applicants should hold a PhD. The position will require the successful candidate to teach up to two undergraduate courses per semester. We are primarily looking for individuals comfortable in teaching introductory micro or macroeconomics, Statistics I and II, and occasionally upper division courses. Review of applications is ongoing throughout the academic year as need arises. Please submit applications via Interfolio. Include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, contact information for three recommenders, recent teaching evaluations (when possible), and other materials as desired. Please contact Johanna Francis, Chair Department of Economics, ajofrancis@fordham.edu if you have any questions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193413": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Macroeconomics or Monetary Economics",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Economics Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses Preferred candidates will have research and teaching interests in Macroeconomics and/or Monetary Economics, with a preference for those with research on open-economy issues. Our department serves a diverse student body at two campuses in New York City: Lincoln Center in Manhattan and Rose Hill in the Bronx. We expect this candidate to teach primarily on the Lincoln Center campus. The chosen candidate will participate in undergraduate and graduate M.A. and Ph.D. Economics programs. Tenure at Fordham requires publication in high-profile journals as well as excellent teaching in our graduate and undergraduate programs. Our department is vibrant and research-focused. Please see our department webpage at https://www.fordham.edu/academics/departments/economics/faculty/ for an overview of the research activities of our faculty members. PhD by August 2027 All application materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/193413 . Please review the position announcement carefully, including the required qualifications, application materials, and submission deadline. Applicants should upload all materials identified below. Cover letter or letter of interest Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement or teaching philosophy Writing samples or selected publications Confidential recommendations (3)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195028": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor in Applied Microeconomics",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Economics Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses Fordham’s Economics Department welcomes applications for a Fall 2027 tenure-track appointment in Applied Microeconomics. While all sub-fields will be considered, we are especially interested in applicants with a specialization in Environmental, International Trade, or Labor Economics. Experience among applied researchers in using different types of big data and modern applied microeconometric tools to facilitate economic decision-making is of particular value. Candidates should also demonstrate a willingness to contribute to the intellectual life and collegial environment of the department and university. Our department serves a diverse student body at two campuses in New York City: Lincoln Center in Manhattan and Rose Hill in the Bronx. We expect this candidate to teach primarily on the Rose Hill campus. The chosen candidate will participate in undergraduate and graduate M.A. and Ph.D. Economics programs. Tenure at Fordham requires publication in high-profile journals as well as excellent teaching in our graduate and undergraduate programs. Our department is vibrant and research-focused. Please see our department webpage for an overview of our faculty members' research activities. PhD by August 2027 All application materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/195028. Please review the position announcement carefully, including the required qualifications, application materials, and submission deadline. Applicants should upload all materials identified below. Cover letter or letter of interest Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement or teaching philosophy Writing samples or selected publications Confidential recommendations (3)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/161696": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Political Science Department",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Political Science Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Political Science Department at Fordham University periodically hires part-time non-tenure track (NTT) faculty to teach introductory and upper-division courses. Based on scheduling needs as well as the qualifications and availability of the applicant, successful candidates may be offered one or two sections of Introduction to Politics and/or introductory and upper-division subfield courses. We regularly hire part-time faculty for fall semester (late August to late December) and spring semester (mid-January to late May). These positions are by semester positions. Part-time NTT instructors at Fordham are represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and certain terms of employment, including minimum per course rates and details concerning benefits, are set by the Adjunct Faculty Collective Bargaining Agreement . For further information, please read our Information for Adjunct Faculty . Our department serves a diverse student body at two campuses in New York City: the Lincoln Center Campus in Manhattan and the Rose Hill Campus in the Bronx. All classes would be taught in person at one of these two campuses, based on departmental needs. Applicants should be an advanced political science graduate student (“all but dissertation”) or ideally hold a PhD in political science. The position will require the successful candidate to teach up to, but not exceeding, two undergraduate courses per semester. Review of applications is ongoing throughout the academic year as need arises. Please submit applications via Interfolio. Include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, contact information for three recommenders, recent teaching evaluations (when possible), and other materials as desired. Please contact the Political Science Department Chair, Dr. Ida Bastiaens, at ibastiaens@fordham.edu , if you have any questions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/162040": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Psychology",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Psychology Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Psychology Department of Fordham University accepts applications for part-time Non-Tenure Track (NTT) faculty to serve as adjunct instructors for psychology courses at our Rose Hill campus in the Bronx and our Lincoln Center campus in Manhattan on a rolling basis. Fordham has a large and vibrant undergraduate community of Psychology majors and minors. In addition, we offer doctoral degrees in Psychometric/Quantitative Psychology, Applied Developmental Psychology, and Clinical Psychology, along with Master’s degree programs in Applied Psychological Methods and Clinical Research Methods. Classes are held in person. Applicants holding a doctoral degree in psychology or a related field are preferred. Part-time NTT instructors at Fordham are represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and certain terms of employment, including per course rates, are set by the Adjunct Faculty Collective Bargaining Agreement. The per-course rate for new instructors as of August 1st, 2024 is $8,500. Part-time NTT faculty may teach a maximum of two courses per semester at either or both of Fordham's two main campuses at Rose Hill (Bronx) and Lincoln Center (Manhattan), based on department need and qualifications. Please note that because we review applications as hiring needs arise, you may not receive an immediate response to your submission. ■ A Terminal degree (PhD or MA) in hand is required for new hires at Fordham. ■ Previous teaching experience at the graduate or undergraduate level is preferred. To apply, please submit a cover letter detailing your teaching interests and experiences, CV, and evidence of teaching effectiveness. Please direct questions to psychology@fordham.edu. For more information about the Psychology department, please visit our website: Psychology Department In many cases, Fordham NTT faculty are eligible for membership in the Services Employees International Union Local 200 United. For NTT faculty benefits information, please visit the Fordham information page for part-time NTT faculty: Part-Time Faculty"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/86333": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Writing and Composition Program Instructor, English",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: English Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campus The English Department of Fordham University accepts applications for part-time Non-Tenure Track (NTT) faculty to teach in its first-year Writing & Composition Program on a rolling basis. Based on scheduling needs as well as qualifications and availability, successful candidates may be offered up to two sections of Composition II, a required Core course that focuses on rhetorical inquiry and research-based writing; or Composition I, which helps students build comfort with college-level writing through attention to writing process, rhetorical reading, and critical language awareness. At Fordham, we view writing as an essential foundation of students’ first-year experience and the undergraduate education more broadly. Our Composition courses aim to introduce diverse forms, purposes, and genres of writing that will help students understand the power of rhetoric and develop their abilities to communicate for a range of academic, professional, and public purposes. Composition courses are generally capped at 16 to support the Jesuit principle of cura personalis , care for the whole person. We seek instructors who are familiar with current, research-based best practices in college writing pedagogy, especially inclusive and antiracist approaches, and who can apply them to foster engaging, equitable teaching and learning environments in concert with Fordham’s Jesuit mission and its strategic plan to educate for racial justice. We strongly encourage candidates to center their writing teaching experience, philosophy, and practice in application materials. Part-time NTT instructors at Fordham are represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and certain terms of employment, including minimum per course rates, are set by the Adjunct Faculty Collective Bargaining Agreement . The per-course rate for new instructors as of August 1st, 2023 is $7,950 . Part-time NTT faculty may teach a maximum of two courses per semester at either or both of Fordham's two main campuses at Rose Hill (Bronx) and Lincoln Center (Manhattan), based on department need. Please note when applying that Rose Hill has the larger student body, and staffing needs are often concentrated there. Candidates are welcome to specify campus preference in their application materials, while noting that priority may be given to those who prioritize Rose Hill or are open to teaching at either campus. On occasion, the English Department hires part-time NTT faculty to teach Texts and Contexts, a Core course in introduction in literary studies and analysis, or more rarely Advanced Literature Core electives. If you are interested in being considered to teach literature courses, please submit tailored materials thru Interfolio. Plese note that because we review applications as hiring needs arise, you may not receive an immediate response to your submission. A Terminal degree (PhD or MFA) in hand is required for new hires at Fordham. A minimum of one year experience teaching first-year writing courses at the college or university level. Experience successfully working with and teaching culturally and linguistically diverse students. Demonstrated commitment to student-centered best practices in the teaching of writing, including revision, peer review, individual conferencing, and more. To apply, please submit a cover letter and CV.  Please direct questions to Crystal Colombini at ccolombini@fordham.edu . For more information about the English department, please visit our website: http://www.fordham.edu/English . In many cases, Fordham NTT faculty are eligible for membership in the Services Employees International Union Local 200 United. For information about NTT faculty benefits, please visit the Fordham information page for part-time NTT faculty: https://www.fordham.edu/info/28368/info_for_adjunct_faculty ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/125978": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty English Department (English Literature)",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: English Location: Rose Hill or Lincoln Center Campus The English Department of Fordham University occasionally hires part-time Non Tenure Track (NTT) faculty to teach literature courses. Based on scheduling needs as well as the qualifications and availability of the applicant, successful candidates may be offered one or two sections of our ENGL2000 Texts and Contexts, a Core course in introduction in literary studies and analysis.  Occasionally we have openings to teach an Advanced Literature Core elective, taught and offered to both English majors and to the general student body as part of our core curriculum, and other upper division electives. We seek instructors who can offer literature electives across a range of areas, who are committed to excellent writing-intensive instruction, and who can foster engaging, equitable teaching and learning environments in consonance with Fordham’s Jesuit mission and its strategic plan to educate for racial justice. At Fordham, Texts and Contexts is required in our Eloquentia Perfecta (E.P. ) writing-intensive core sequence, and we view instruction in writing, rhetoric, and close textual analysis as an essential foundation of students’ experience in both literature courses and the undergraduate curriculum more broadly. \"Text and Contexts\" is capped at 19 students, and students must complete 15 pages of polished writing with assignments that include scaffolded cycles of feedback and revision. Instructors must build writing and speaking into the fabric of their assignments and class meetings, and 20% of class time must be devoted to instruction in writing and speaking. Texts and Contexts sections have subtitles, and readings grouped around a broad theme (for example, \"New York City in Literature,\" or \"Medieval Monsters and Marvels\"). We are especially interested in hiring instructors who can design engaging Texts & Context courses that meet department requirements, draw student enrollment, and attract students to the value of the English major and minor degrees; we, therefore, encourage you to accent these matters in your application materials. Occasionally we have Advanced Literature Core electives available that tend to be 35 students; most literature electives are 35 students and meet twice a week. We will not be sending applicants a formal acknowledgement that their materials were received but will be in touch if a section or position does open up. Please do not send duplicates of your materials to departmental emails. *See above right for per course rate. A Terminal degree (PhD) in hand is preferred. A minimum of one year's experience teaching literary or cultural studies at the college or university level. Experience successfully working with and teaching culturally and linguistically diverse students. Evidence of experience in the teaching of writing as part of the teaching of literature. The English Department at Fordham University operates coherently across two campuses in New York City: The Rose Hill campus in the Bronx and the Lincoln Center campus in Manhattan. NTT faculty may be offered courses at either campus depending on scheduling needs. If applicants are interested in having their qualifications for Composition 1 and Composition 2 reviewed in the future, they are welcome to indicate that in their cover letter. However, since Composition instructors are hired more frequently and with primary attention to knowledge of rhetoric and writing research and pedagogy, we encourage you to submit tailored application materials to that separate Interfolio portal . To apply, please submit a cover letter and CV.  Please direct questions to calvino3@fordham.edu . Fordham is an independent, Catholic University in the Jesuit tradition committed to the idea that cultural and intellectual diversity is central to the excellence of our academic programs and our institutional vitality.  Diversifying its faculty and curriculum is at the heart of the mission and vision of Fordham University. As an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, we welcome applications from people of all backgrounds. Women, people of color, veterans, and people with disabilities are especially encouraged to apply. We are committed to an application process that is accessible to individuals with disabilities, and we encourage applicants to request any needed accommodation(s). For more information about the English department, please visit our website: http://www.fordham.edu/English . In many cases, Fordham NTT faculty are eligible for membership in the Services Employees International Union Local 200 United. For information about NTT faculty benefits, please visit the Fordham information page for part-time NTT faculty http://apply.interfolio.com/125978"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/135278": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Philosophy Department",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Philosophy Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Philosophy Department of Fordham University accepts applications for part-time Non-Tenure Track (NTT) faculty to teach in its \"Philosophy of Human Nature\" and \"Philosophical Ethics\" classes on a rolling basis. Based on scheduling needs as well as qualifications and availability, successful candidates may be offered up to two sections of \"Philosophy of Human Nature,\" a required Core course that focuses on central metaphysical and epistemological questions surrounding human nature, with readings from classical thinkers such as Plato and Descartes along with thinkers from under-represented groups in philosophy; or \"Philosophical Ethics,\" which focuses on major normative ethical theories underlying moral decision-making in our everyday lives, with readings from classical thinkers such as Aristotle and Kant along with thinkers from under-represented groups in philosophy. We seek instructors who can foster engaging, equitable teaching and learning environments in concert with Fordham’s Jesuit mission. Part-time NTT instructors at Fordham are represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU Local 200), and specific terms of employment, including minimum per-course rates, are set by the Adjunct Faculty Collective Bargaining Agreement .  Please visit the following website for further information: Information for Adjunct Faculty. Part-time NTT faculty may teach a maximum of two courses per semester at either or both of Fordham's two main campuses at Rose Hill (Bronx) and Lincoln Center (Manhattan), based on department need. Please note when applying that Rose Hill has a larger student body, and staffing needs are often concentrated there. Candidates are welcome to specify campus preference in their application materials while noting that priority may be given to those who prioritize Rose Hill or are open to teaching at either campus. Please note that you may not receive an immediate response to your submission because we review applications as hiring needs arise. A terminal degree in Philosophy (PhD or MA) is required for new teaching hires in Philosophy. A minimum of one year of teaching experience at the college or university level. Experience successfully working with and teaching culturally and linguistically diverse students. Demonstrated commitment to student-centered best practices in the teaching of Philosophy. To apply, please submit (i) a cover letter, (ii) a CV, and (iii) evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as recent teaching evaluations or a teaching dossier.  Please direct questions to Stephen R. Grimm at sgrimm@fordham.edu . For more information about the Philosophy department, please visit our website: https://www.fordham.edu/academics/departments/philosophy."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194143": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor, Political Sociology, Department of Sociology and Anthropology",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Sociology and Anthropology Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Department of Sociology and Anthropology at Fordham University invites applications for the position of full-time, tenure-track assistant professor in sociology. We seek a scholar whose research focuses on political sociology and who has demonstrated the ability to teach courses in this area. The position will be located primarily at Fordham College’s Lincoln Center campus (in Manhattan) with the possibility of future service and teaching at the Rose Hill campus (in the Bronx) or in interdisciplinary programs. Department Overview: https://www.fordham.edu/academics/departments/sociology-and-anthropology/ Candidates must have a Ph.D. in hand at the time of appointment. Demonstrated excellence in teaching at the college level and an active program of research and publication are required. Applications should be submitted online via Interfolio and include a cover letter, CV, a writing sample (no more than 35 pages), peer and student teaching evaluations, if available, sample syllabi, and the names and contact information for three references. Applications received by October 15, 2026, will receive full consideration. Please direct inquiries to Professor Christopher Rhomberg (rhomberg@fordham.edu). Preferences will be given to candidates with strengths in political economy and a focus on the United States in a global context. The ability to teach quantitative methods at the undergraduate level may also be considered. The department is especially interested in qualified candidates who are committed to cultural and intellectual diversity and will actively contribute to our efforts toward advancing equity and inclusion through their research, teaching, and service. All application materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/194143 . Please review the position announcement carefully, including the required qualifications, application materials, and submission deadline. Applicants should upload all materials identified below. Cover letter or letter of interest Curriculum vitae Writing samples or selected publications Sample syllabi Teaching evaluations Confidential recommendations (3)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195063": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor, Financial Economics",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Gabelli School of Business: Finance and Business Economics Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Finance and Business Economics Area of Fordham University’s Gabelli School of Business invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in Finance beginning September 1, 2027. The position is open to candidates at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. The base salary range is: Assistant, $200,000-$250,000; Associate, $250,000–$275,000, commensurate with rank and qualifications. Competitive summer research support is also provided. Candidates from all areas of finance will be considered. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active research agenda, teach undergraduate, graduate, and/or doctoral courses, advise and mentor students, and participate in the intellectual and service activities of the Area and the Gabelli School of Business. Fordham University is an independent, Catholic university in the Jesuit tradition, located on two main campuses in New York City. The Gabelli School of Business is accredited by the AACSB. Finance is a strategically important discipline within the Gabelli School and has grown considerably in both research productivity and course offerings in recent years. The Finance and Business Economics Area offers a broad range of undergraduate and graduate courses and provides an active environment for faculty research and intellectual exchange. Consistent with Fordham’s Jesuit tradition, we value cultural and intellectual diversity and strive to create an academic community and campus culture that attracts and facilitates faculty development. Additional information about the Finance and Business Economics Area and its faculty is available at https://www.fordham.edu/gabelli-school-of-business/faculty/academic-areas/finance-and-business-economics/finance-faculty/?utm_source=chatgpt.com. Applicants must have a Ph.D. or an equivalent degree in Finance, Economics, or a directly related field, or expect to earn the degree within one semester of the start of the appointment. Candidates must demonstrate strong potential for high-quality research and publication in leading finance journals, as well as a commitment to excellence in teaching. Candidates with prior faculty experience should have a demonstrated record of research productivity and effective teaching. All application materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio at this link:https://apply.interfolio.com/195063 https://apply.interfolio.com/195063 . Please review the position announcement carefully, including the required qualifications, application materials, and submission deadline. Applicants should upload all materials identified below. Cover letter or letter of interest Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement or teaching philosophy Writing samples or selected publications Confidential recommendations (3)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193415": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Biological/Chemical Basis of Urban Environmental Health, Department of Natural Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Natural Sciences Location: Lincoln Center Campus The Department of Natural Sciences invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year at Fordham University Lincoln Center campus. We seek an innovative scholar whose research leverages unique opportunities to understand how aspects of urban environments interact with biological processes to impact health outcomes. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Biology, Chemistry, Environmental Science, or a closely related discipline and demonstrate a strong, proven excellence in undergraduate teaching and student mentoring. Priority will be given to candidates who conduct laboratory or field research relevant to New York City and its communities, and who maintain a strong research record of engaging undergraduate students in active research. Tenure-track faculty are expected to teach undergraduate lectures and laboratory courses across the Natural Sciences curriculum and to develop new coursework at the intersection of urban environmental science and public health. Ph.D. in Biology, Chemistry, Environmental Science, or a closely related discipline; demonstrate a strong, proven excellence in undergraduate teaching and student mentoring. All application materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/193415 . Please review the position announcement carefully, including the required qualifications, application materials, and submission deadline. Applicants should upload all materials identified below. Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement or teaching philosophy Confidential recommendations (3)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192150": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor, Music (Musicology or Ethnomusicology), African and/or African Diasporic Musics",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Art History & Music Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Department of Art History and Music at Fordham University seeks a full-time tenure-track Ethnomusicologist / Musicologist at the rank of Assistant Professor, with a specialization in African-American, African, and/or Afro-Diasporic Musics.  The position, based at our Rose Hill Campus in the Bronx, begins in Fall 2027.  Candidates should possess:  a terminal degree (Ph.D.) in musicology or ethnomusicology; a dynamic research program; and demonstrated effectiveness as a classroom teacher. The successful candidate will be responsible for advanced courses directed toward upper-level students in Music. In addition to the primary field of expertise, subspecialties (such as contemporary music production/industry or musical theater) are warmly encouraged. The candidate will also be responsible for introductory courses, aimed at the general student population, in African and/or African-Diasporic musics.  The successful candidate will be strongly encouraged to work closely with other academic departments and programs at Fordham (such as African and African American Studies, American Studies, Communication and Media Studies), and to integrate NYC-area cultural institutions into their teaching. PhD in Ethnomusicology or Musicology Review of applications will begin on Friday, 2 October 2026. Candidates should submit via Interfolio:  a Letter of Application (describing the candidate’s research and pedagogical program), a CV, three letters of reference, and a writing sample (article, book chapter, or equivalent). We ask all applicants to submit the following materials for consideration: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Confidential Recommendations (3) Please submit the requested documents via Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/192150"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192009": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor, Accounting & Taxation, Gabelli School of Business",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Gabelli School of Business: Accounting and Taxation Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Accounting and Taxation area of Fordham University’s Gabelli School of Business is seeking applications for one entry-level tenure-track assistant professor position beginning September 1, 2027. Research support is provided for junior faculty through summer research grants and reduced teaching loads. Applicants are expected to have received their PhD by Fall 2027. The Gabelli School of Business is a strong and well-regarded business school. It consistently ranks highly in national rankings for undergraduate programs (#21 in the nation) and graduate programs, especially for its MBA (#10 in the nation for the Accounting Specialty Area) and EMBA (#10 in the nation). As the Gabelli School emphasizes both research and teaching, all candidates need to be able to work with students from a range of social, cultural, and economic backgrounds, have strong communication skills, and be committed to mentoring and serving as a role model for underrepresented students. Having a diverse and inclusive community of students and faculty is a key part of Fordham's Strategic Plan. Applicants should demonstrate strong research training and the potential to publish in top-tier academic journals in accounting and related fields. While we will consider candidates in any accounting-related sub-specialty, we are particularly interested in those who (i) have the ability to teach various tax courses (both undergraduate and graduate) and/or upper-level accounting courses at a level that proactively supports students seeking licensure and credentials for their careers, and (ii) have an understanding of how AI and big data are impacting financial reporting and research. All application materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/192009 . Please review the position announcement carefully, including the required qualifications, application materials, and submission deadline. Applicants should upload all materials identified below. Cover letter or letter of interest Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement or teaching philosophy Writing samples or selected publications Confidential recommendations (3)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/165390": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Intro to Fashion Design, Department of Theatre and Visual Arts",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Theatre and Visual Arts Location: Lincoln Center Campus Fordham University seeks a skilled, experienced part-time non-tenure-track adjunct faculty to teach Introduction to Fashion Design, a required course for the Fashion Studies Minor, for the Fall 2025 semester. Fordham Theatre is committed to cultivating an expansive vision for the program that assures its preeminent standing, elevates its profile as an innovative leader in undergraduate theater training, and centers social justice in all aspects of its work. The Theatre Program is based at Fordham’s Lincoln Center Campus in Manhattan and is housed academically within a department alongside Visual Art. The Fashion Studies Minor is a popular interdisciplinary program with a curriculum shared by faculty in the College of Arts and Sciences and Gabelli Business School. It weaves together the industry's cultural, creative, and commercial components. Responsibilities include guiding students in fashion design principles and techniques, preparing course materials and assessments aligned with the curriculum, conducting lectures and workshops, mentoring students while fostering a creative learning environment, and bringing industry experience of current trends into the classroom. This professor teaches two courses: Tuesdays 11:30am-2:15 PM and Tuesdays 2:30-5:15 PM beginning Fall 2025 (semester runs August 27-December 9, 2025) Course Description: THEA 1210 - Introduction to Fashion Design This course explores the art and the business of fashion design by tracing its history in Europe and America, understanding the contribution of fibers to the medium, communicating design details through several modes of drawing, and investigating the market factors that shape the contemporary fashion industry around the globe. The Service Employees International (Local200) represents part-time NTT instructors at Fordham Union (SEIU), and specific terms of employment, including minimum per-course rates, are set by the Collective Bargaining Agreement . The per-course rate for new instructors as of March 24, 2025 is $8,500. Part-time NTT faculty may teach a maximum of two courses per semester at either or both of Fordham's two main campuses at Rose Hill (Bronx) and Lincoln Center (Manhattan). Intro to Fashion Design is currently only offered at the Lincoln Center campus. A Terminal degree (PhD or MFA) in fashion design, or a related field A minimum of one year experience teaching relative subject matter at the college or university level. Experience successfully working with and teaching culturally and linguistically diverse students. Demonstrated commitment to student-centered teaching and has a joy and passion in sharing knowledge and learning with students. To apply, please submit a cover letter and CV using Interfolio. Questions may be directed to the Director of the Theatre Program, May Adrales, at madrales@fordham.edu or the Director of Fashion Studies, Robert Hernandez, rhernandez57@fordham.edu, with the subject line “Intro to Fashion Design.”"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/171393": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Playwriting, Theatre and Visual Arts",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Theatre and Visual Arts Location: Lincoln Center Campus Fordham University seeks a skilled, experienced part-time non-tenure track adjunct faculty to teach Playwriting , a required course for the playwrights and a major elective within the Theatre Program, and/or co-teach a foundational theatre major course, Collaboration , a mandatory course for incoming first-years in the program for Fall 2025 Fordham Theatre is committed to cultivating an expansive vision for the program that assures its preeminent standing, elevates its profile as an innovative leader in undergraduate theater training, and centers social justice in all aspects of its work. The Theatre Program is based at Fordham’s Lincoln Center Campus in Manhattan and is housed academically within a department alongside Visual Art. Responsibilities include guiding students in theatre principles and techniques, preparing course materials and assessments aligned with the curriculum, conducting lectures and workshops, mentoring students while fostering a creative learning environment, and bringing industry experience of current trends into the classroom. This professor may teach up to two courses: Collaboration I, Mondays and Wednesdays 11:00-12:45 pm; and/or Playwriting, Tuesdays 11:30 am-2:15 pm beginning Fall 2025 (semester runs August 27-December 9, 2025) Course Description:  THEA 3700 - Playwriting This playwriting workshop is the cornerstone of the playwriting program. It intentionally welcomes writers of many levels of experience to one dynamic space. The workshop's goals are to teach basic craft and create an environment that will guide the writers' explanation of their individual voices. We concentrate on four major issues: storytelling, character, structure, and language. Course Description: THEA 2080: Collaboration I This course is designed to provide the student with 1) tools for effective and meaningful collaboration, including strengthening communication skills, critical thinking skills and creative problem solving skills; 2) a basic understanding of acting, directing, designing, stage management and playwriting; 3) opportunities for hands-on experience in all disciplines; 4) opportunities for how to give and receive critical feedback and offer meaningful reflection of theatrical practices. Part-time NTT instructors at Fordham are represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU). Certain employment terms, including minimum per-course rates, are set by the Adjunct Faculty Collective Bargaining Agreement . Part-time NTT faculty may teach a maximum of two courses per semester at either or both of Fordham's two main campuses at Rose Hill (Bronx) and Lincoln Center (Manhattan). Playwriting and Collaboration I is currently only offered at the Lincoln Center campus. A Terminal degree (MFA in Theatre, specifically in Playwriting). A minimum of one year of experience teaching related subject matter at the college or university level. Experience successfully working with and teaching culturally and linguistically diverse students from a range of theatrical experiences Demonstrated commitment to student-centered teaching and has a joy and passion in sharing knowledge and learning with students. To apply, please submit a cover letter and CV.  Questions may be directed to the Director of the Theatre Program, May Adrales at madrales@fordham.edu with the subject line “Playwriting Instructor.” Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/177500": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Visual Arts",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Theatre and Visual Arts Location: Lincoln Center Campus The Theatre and Visual Arts Department at Fordham University periodically hires part-time, non-tenure-track (NTT) faculty to teach introductory and upper-division courses in the following areas: Art and Engagement, Built Environment, Film and Video, Graphic Design, Painting and Drawing, and Photography. Based on scheduling needs as well as the qualifications and availability of the applicant. Part-time NTT instructors at Fordham are represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU), and certain terms of employment, including minimum per-course rates and details concerning benefits, are set by the Adjunct Faculty Collective Bargaining Agreement. For further information, please read our Information for Adjunct Faculty. The Visual Arts Program serves a diverse student body at two campuses in New York City: the Lincoln Center Campus in Manhattan and the Rose Hill Campus in the Bronx. All classes would be taught in person at one of these two campuses, based on departmental needs. Applicants should have a terminal degree in their respective fields. The position will require the successful candidate to teach up to, but not exceeding, two undergraduate courses per semester. Review of applications is ongoing throughout the academic year as need arises. Please submit applications via Interfolio. Include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, contact information for three recommenders, recent teaching evaluations (when possible), and other materials as desired. Please contact the Visual Arts Program Head, Stephan Apicella-Hitchcock at apicellahit@fordham.edu or Theatre and Visual Arts Department Chair, Dr. Fadi Skeiker, or if you have any questions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/178074": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Theatre",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Theatre and Visual Arts Location: Lincoln Center Campus The Theatre and Visual Arts Department at Fordham University periodically hires part-time, non-tenure-track (NTT) faculty to teach introductory and upper-division courses in areas such as Performance, Design and Production, Playwriting, and Directing. Based on scheduling needs as well as the qualifications and availability of the applicant. Part-time NTT instructors at Fordham are represented by the Service Employees International Union (SEIU), and certain terms of employment, including minimum per-course rates and details concerning benefits, are set by the Adjunct Faculty Collective Bargaining Agreement. For further information, please read our Information for Adjunct Faculty. The Theatre Program serves a diverse student body at two campuses in New York City: the Lincoln Center Campus in Manhattan and the Rose Hill Campus in the Bronx. All classes would be taught in person at one of these two campuses, based on departmental needs. Learn more about the program and curriculum on the website (https://www.fordham.edu/academics/departments/theatre/) Applicants should have a terminal degree in their respective fields. The position will require the successful candidate to teach up to, but not exceeding, two undergraduate courses per semester. Review of applications is ongoing throughout the academic year as need arises. Please submit applications via Interfolio. Include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, contact information for three recommenders, recent teaching evaluations (when possible), and other materials as desired. Please contact the Theatre Program Head, May Adrales, at madrales@fordham.edu or the Theatre and Visual Arts Department Chair, Dr. Fadi Skeiker, at fskeiker@fordham.edu if you have any questions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192146": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor, Theatre Design",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Arts & Sciences: Theatre and Visual Arts Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses The Department of Theatre and Visual Arts at Fordham University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor in Theatre Design at the Lincoln Center campus beginning in Fall 2027. Fordham’s Theatre Program engages across a broad spectrum of embodied practices and rigorous study within four areas of concentration: Design and Production, Performance, Directing, and Playwriting. We seek a collaborative artist-educator with expertise in theatre design. The Design and Production Concentration houses students in the set, light, sound, costume, and stage management concentrations. The successful candidate will demonstrate the ability to teach across multiple areas of theatrical design. Experience teaching and/or professional practice in lighting and sound design is also highly desirable. Applicants with exceptional qualifications in a single design area who demonstrate a commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration and teaching across the curriculum are encouraged to apply. We invite applicants who are invested in continuing to generate and support future generations of innovative artists and creative risk-takers through their own dynamic experiences within the department’s intersecting areas of study. In service of Fordham’s Jesuit mission, particularly its commitment to educating discerning students dedicated to the pursuit of social justice, we welcome candidates interested in developing the Theatre Program’s partnerships with community-based organizations and contributing to the interdisciplinary conversation on theatre and global citizenship. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate courses in theatre design, mentor student designers, and contribute to developing an innovative, inclusive, and forward-looking curriculum that prepares students for contemporary artistic practice. In addition to teaching responsibilities, the faculty member will serve as a resident designer for the department, designing at least one mainstage production each academic year and supervising student-designed Main Stage and Studio productions. Fordham University is committed to excellence through diversity and welcomes applications from individuals who will contribute to the intellectual, artistic, and cultural vitality of our community. For inquiries about the position, please contact Dr. Fadi Skeiker, Chair of the Department of Theatre and Visual Arts at fskeiker@fordham.edu Candidates should demonstrate a strong record of professional creative activity, a commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching and mentorship, and the ability to contribute to an inclusive learning environment. The successful candidate is expected to maintain an active professional practice while engaging in scholarly and/or creative work appropriate for a tenure-track appointment. A terminal degree (MFA or PhD, as appropriate to the discipline) is required by the time of appointment. Applicants should possess a terminal degree and 3-5 years of college-level teaching and professional experience. Demonstrated successful teaching and mentorship experience in academic or studio environments. Ability to contribute to curriculum development for theatre studies in a liberal arts environment. Demonstrated collaboration and leadership skills that include excellent written and oral communication, effective management skills, and innovation and follow-through. Demonstrated leadership in areas contributing to diversity, equity, and inclusion, as well as a commitment to advancing these principles and the principles of Jesuit education within the Department and the School of Arts & Sciences. Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026, with initial Zoom interviews scheduled in mid-October and campus visits taking place right after. Applications will continue to be accepted and considered until the position is filled. We ask all applicants to submit the following materials for consideration: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Art Portfolio/Sample Confidential Recommendations (3) Please submit the requested documents via Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/192146"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192013": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track: Assistant Professor, Information, Technology & Operations, Gabelli School of Business",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Gabelli School of Business: Information, Technology and Operations Location: Rose Hill and Lincoln Center Campuses Expertise aligns with the School's strategic initiatives in artificial intelligence and emergency technology. remove data management and programming. The Information, Technology, and Operations (ITO) Area at the Gabelli School of Business at Fordham University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning in Fall 2027. Applicants should hold a Ph.D. or expect to complete one by the appointment date in Information Systems, Business Analytics, Artificial Intelligence, or a closely related field. Candidates should demonstrate a strong record of research published in, or the potential to publish in, leading academic journals. We particularly welcome candidates whose research and teaching expertise align with the Gabelli School's strategic initiatives in artificial intelligence. The ability to teach technical AI-related courses is highly desirable. The successful candidate will develop and sustain a high-impact research program, publish in leading academic journals, teach undergraduate and graduate courses, contribute to curriculum development, and participate in departmental, School, and University service. The candidate is also expected to engage in interdisciplinary research and foster collaborations with academic, industry, and community partners. The Information Technology and Operations (ITO) Area at Fordham University's Gabelli School of Business is a collaborative community of scholars advancing research and education in artificial intelligence, information systems, business analytics, and operations management. Located at the Lincoln Center and Rose Hill campuses in New York City, the area offers undergraduate, MBA, and doctoral programs, as well as three specialized master's programs: the M.S. in Business Analytics (MSBA), M.S. in Information Technology (MSIT), and M.S. in AI in Business (MSAIB). Faculty maintain active research programs across AI, analytics, digital transformation, and related fields, fostering a supportive environment for impactful scholarship, interdisciplinary collaboration, and innovation. Applicants should hold a Ph.D. or expect to complete one by the appointment date in Information Systems, Business Analytics, Artificial Intelligence, or a closely related field. Reference will be given to candidates with strong potential to publish in leading Information Systems and related academic journals. Candidates should also demonstrate the ability to teach and develop technically rigorous courses in artificial intelligence, machine learning, generative AI, and other emerging technologies. All application materials must be submitted electronically through Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/192013 . Please review the position announcement carefully, including the required qualifications, application materials, and submission deadline. Applicants should upload all materials identified below. Cover letter or letter of interest Curriculum vitae Research statement Teaching statement or teaching philosophy Writing samples or selected publications Contact References (3)"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194516": {
      "title": "*Tenure-Track Faculty: Literacy/English Language Arts Education, Graduate School of Education",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Education: Curriculum & Teaching Location: Lincoln Center Campus The Division of Curriculum and Teaching (C&T) at Fordham University’s Graduate School of Education (GSE) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in Literacy/English Language Arts (ELA) Education. The position begins in September 2027. The successful candidate will teach and mentor students across programs in Adolescent English Language Arts Education, Childhood Education, and the Ph.D. program in Innovation in Curriculum and Instruction (ICI). We seek a scholar with a strong record of, or demonstrated potential for, excellence in research, teaching, and service, and a deep commitment to social justice and urban education. We are particularly interested in candidates whose scholarship and teaching engage issues relevant to contemporary literacy/ELA education. The successful candidate will contribute to the C&T Division and Graduate School through an active research agenda, high-quality teaching, doctoral and master’s student mentorship, program leadership, and collaborative service. Research Maintain an active and visible research agenda in Literacy/English Language Arts education, or a closely related area. Publish in peer-reviewed journals and seek external funding. Engage in interdisciplinary and collaborative research with colleagues within and beyond C&T and GSE. Teaching & Advising Teach graduate courses in Literacy/ELA education at the master’s and doctoral levels. Utilize diverse teaching modalities, including in-person, hybrid, hyflex, synchronous, and asynchronous formats. Advise and mentor both master’s and doctoral students. Teach primarily at Fordham’s Lincoln Center Campus in Manhattan, NY. Program Leadership Serve as Program Coordinator for the Adolescent ELA program and provide leadership in literacy/ELA education across relevant childhood programs. Recruit, advise, and support teacher candidates and doctoral students in Literacy/ELA Education. Review applications and participate in admissions and recruitment efforts (e.g., Information Sessions). Identify, mentor, and evaluate adjunct faculty in Literacy/ELA Education Collaborate on accreditation activities, including data collection and reporting. Represent the ELA program at Divisional and school-wide meetings and initiatives. The Division of Curriculum and Teaching is accredited by the Association for Advancing Quality in Educator Preparation (AAQEP). The Graduate School of Education is deeply engaged in urban education, inclusive pedagogy, and community partnerships and is committed to fostering a diverse and excellent academic community. Required Qualifications Doctoral degree in Literacy/ELA Education or a closely related field (ABD candidates will be considered with expected completion by August 2027) Demonstrated understanding of and commitment to social justice and urban education, with evidence of incorporating principles of equity and inclusion into teaching, scholarship, and/or service At least three years of K-12 teaching experience in literacy, ELA, or a closely related area Preferred Qualifications Current or previous teacher certification/licensure in literacy, ELA, or a related field Experience in academic program coordination and leadership Experience teaching in teacher preparation programs, including online and/or hybrid modalities Record of peer-reviewed scholarship and demonstrated potential for continued scholarly productivity and pursuit of external funding. Expertise in Literacy/ELA Education or a related area, such as reading and writing instruction, adolescent literacy, literacy assessment and intervention, multilingual learners and literacy, transliteracy/multiliteracies, digital literacy, or literacy across the curriculum Experience mentoring graduate students and/or participating in doctoral research and dissertation work Please submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, research statement, teaching statement, and contact information for three references through Interfolio ( https://apply.interfolio.com/194516 ). Review of applications will begin October 25, 2026. Screening interviews are expected to begin in Early-November 2026. Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. For more information, contact Dr. Su-Je Cho at scho@fordham.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/139317": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Entertainment Law, School of Law",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Law Location: Lincoln Center Campus Fordham Law School invites applications for an adjunct instructor to teach a course in Entertainment Law.  The expected start date is for the fall 2024 semester. Responsibilities: Teach an engaging course at the Law School exploring the legal issues and business practices within the broad realm of the entertainment industry. This includes a comprehensive exploration of relevant case law, statutes, and representative agreements. Develop a syllabus spanning the Entertainment Law landscape, encompassing key areas such as talent representation, recorded music, music publishing, motion pictures and television, and video programming services. Shape a set of learning objectives that students will have mastered by the end of the course. Evaluate and grade student course performance in alignment with the appropriate grading curves, providing constructive feedback to foster students’ understanding of core concepts. Juris Doctor degree with a specialization or extensive expertise in Entertainment Law. Prior teaching experience preferred. The salary range for this job is $2,000-$9,000 per semester, based on a number of factors including but not limited to the number of credits offered, experience, practice area, training, teaching modality, and expected enrollment. We value diversity and strongly encourage individuals from a range of backgrounds to apply. Fordham Law School is a national leader in the field, with approximately 1,600 students in our J.D., LL.M. and M.S.L. programs. Fordham is the Jesuit University of New York and serves students of all faiths, including those with no faith. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and a brief statement outlining their approach to teaching Entertainment Law."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/140903": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: School of Law",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Law Location: Lincoln Center Campus Fordham Law School invites applications for an adjunct instructor position to teach a Law course in a wide range of subject areas.  The Law School is particularly interested in courses that combine both theory and current legal practice. Responsibilities: Teach an engaging course at the Law School exploring legal issues within a chosen subject area, including in-depth analysis of case law, statutes, regulations, and practice-oriented documents (contracts, pleadings, briefs, etc.) Develop a thirteen-week syllabus that covers essential topics within a chosen field or domain. Foster an inclusive and collaborative learning environment conducive to critical thinking, discussion, and debate. Shape a set of learning objectives that students will master by the end of the course. Evaluate and grade student course performance in alignment with the appropriate grading curves, providing constructive feedback to foster students’ understanding of core concepts. Juris Doctor degree with a specialization in proposed subject area. Prior teaching experience preferred. The salary range for this job is $2,000-$15,000 per semester, dependent on the number of credits offered, experience, and practice area. We value diversity and strongly encourage individuals from a range of backgrounds to apply. Fordham Law School is a national leader in the field, with approximately 1,600 students in our J.D., LL.M. and M.S.L. programs. Fordham is the Jesuit University of New York and serves students of all faiths, including those with no faith. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and a brief statement outlining their approach to teaching and the topic and goals of their proposed course(s)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/142930": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: FDA, School of Law",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Law Location: Lincoln Center Campus Fordham Law School invites applications for an adjunct instructor position to teach a Law course in Food and Drug Administration (FDA) Law. The expected start date is for the fall 2024 or spring 2025 semester.  The Law School is particularly interested in courses that combine both theory and current legal practice. Responsibilities: Teach an engaging course at the Law School exploring legal issues within FDA law, including in-depth analysis of statues, regulations, case-law, FDA-guidances, and other practice-oriented documents. Develop a syllabus that covers essential topics within FDA law, including topics such as the Food, Drug and Cosmetic Act, regulatory procedures relating to drug, biologic, and device approval, post-market regulation, and industry best practices. Shape a set of learning objectives that students will master by the end of the course. Evaluate and grade student course performance in alignment with the appropriate grading curves, providing constructive feedback to foster students’ understanding of core concepts. Juris Doctor degree with extensive expertise in FDA Law Prior teaching experience preferred The salary range for this job is $2,000-$15,000 per semester, dependent on the number of credits offered, experience and practice area. We value diversity and strongly encourage individuals from a range of backgrounds to apply. Fordham Law School is a national leader in the field, with approximately 1,600 students in our J.D., LL.M. and M.S.L. programs. Fordham is the Jesuit University of New York and serves students of all faiths, including those with no faith. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and a brief statement outlining their approach to teaching and the topic and goals of their proposed course(s) at this link (http://apply.interfolio.com/142930).  For questions, please contact Audrey Glassman at aglassman@law.fordham.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/142931": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Biotechnology Law, School of Law",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Law Location: Lincoln Center Campus Fordham Law School invites applications for an adjunct instructor position to teach a course on law and regulation in biotechnology. The expected start date is for the fall 2024 or spring 2025 semester.  The Law School is particularly interested in courses that combine both theory and current legal practice. Responsibilities: Teach an engaging course at the Law School exploring legal issues within biotechnology, including in-depth analysis of statues, regulations, case-law, and other practice-oriented documents as well as industry practices. Develop a syllabus that covers essential topics throughout the life cycle of biotechnological products, from early-stage research to clinical trials, approval, and post-approval marketing and safety. Shape a set of learning objectives that students will master by the end of the course. Evaluate and grade student course performance in alignment with the appropriate grading curves, providing constructive feedback to foster students’ understanding of core concepts. R3. Law and Regulation in Biotechnology Course Description This course serves as an introduction to the intricate legal and regulatory landscape governing the biotechnology industry. Students will explore the various legal frameworks that shape the entire life cycle of biotechnological products, from early-stage research to post-approval marketing and safety. The course will delve into essential topics, including the clinical trials process, good manufacturing practices, FDA approval requirements, and regulations concerning generics and biosimilars. Additionally, the course will survey the legal considerations on the business and financing side of biotechnology, covering intellectual property, funding mechanisms for research, material transfer agreements, other contractual arrangements, and various corporate structures. Learning Objectives Comprehensive understanding of biotechnology principles: define and explain the fundamental principles and processes involved in biotechnology, including genetic engineering, molecular biology, and other key technologies. Knowledge of regulatory agencies and legal frameworks: identify and describe the roles and responsibilities of regulatory agencies governing the biotechnology industry and analyze the legal frameworks that shape the development, approval, and marketing of biotechnological products. Understand intellectual property, clinical trials, and FDA approval processes. Apply legal principles to real-world scenarios and case studies. Develop critical thinking and problem-solving skills in navigating complex legal issues in the biotechnology industry. Evaluation 10% Class participation 40% Three problem sets 50% Final exam Assignment Topics Week 1: Introduction to biotechnology and regulatory frameworks Overview of the biotechnology industry Regulatory agencies and their roles Key legal principles governing biotechnology Week 2: Early-stage research and intellectual property Contractual considerations in early-stage research Regulatory considerations in early-stage research Protection of biotechnological inventions Patents, trade secrets and other forms of intellectual property Balancing innovation and disclosure Week 3: Clinical trials process Regulatory requirements for conducting clinical trials Informed consent and ethical considerations Data collection, monitoring, and reporting obligations Week 4: Good manufacturing practices (GMP) Ensuring product quality and safety in manufacturing Regulatory compliance and inspections GMP’s role in biotechnological product development Week 5: FDA approval requirements New Drug Application (NDA) and Biologics License Application (BLA) processes Regulatory pathways for approval Expedited approval programs and breakthrough designations Week 6: Post-approval marketing and safety Drug and device labeling requirements Post-marketing surveillance and adverse event reporting Risk management plans and post-approval commitments Week 7: Generics and biosimilars Regulatory pathways for generic drugs and biosimilars Interchangeability and substitution considerations Legal challenges and market dynamics Week 8: Business and financing in biotechnology Funding mechanisms for biotechnological research Material transfer agreements and other contractual agreements Corporate structures and governance in biotechnology companies Week 9: Ethical and social implications Ethical considerations in biotechnological research and development Societal impact and public perception Legal responses to ethical challenges Week 10: Emerging issues in biotechnology law CRISPR technology and gene editing Personalized medicine and regulatory challenges Global harmonization and international collaborations ·       Juris Doctor degree with extensive expertise in biotechnology law. ·       Prior teaching experience preferred. The salary range for this job is $2,000-$15,000 per semester, dependent on the number of credits offered, experience and practice area. We value diversity and strongly encourage individuals from a range of backgrounds to apply. Fordham Law School is a national leader in the field, with approximately 1,600 students in our J.D., LL.M. and M.S.L. programs. Fordham is the Jesuit University of New York and serves students of all faiths, including those with no faith. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and a brief statement outlining their approach to teaching and the topic and goals of their proposed course(s) at this link (http://apply.interfolio.com/142931).  For questions, please contact Audrey Glassman at aglassman@law.fordham.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/145727": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Professional Responsibility (LLM) Course",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Law Location: Lincoln Center Campus Fordham Law School invites applications for an adjunct instructor to teach a course in professional responsibility for foreign-trained LL.M. students.  The expected start date is for the fall 2024 semester. Responsibilities : Using a textbook, the ABA Model Rules of Professional Conduct, and the NY Rules of Professional Conduct, supplemented by pertinent cases and other materials, this course will examine the fundamental notions that govern the conduct of lawyers in relation to their clients, third parties and the courts. Provide a holistic introduction to some of the key professional responsibility aspects that a foreign law-trained lawyer should expect to consider when working in the U.S. or dealing with U.S. lawyers from abroad. Examine the fundamental notions that govern the conduct of lawyers in relation to their clients, third parties and the courts. Develop a syllabus using a textbook, the ABA Model Rules of Professional Conduct, and the NY Rules of Professional Conduct, supplemented by pertinent cases and other materials. Shape a set of learning objectives that students will have mastered by the end of the course. Evaluate and grade student course performance in alignment with the appropriate grading curves, providing constructive feedback to foster students’ understanding of core concepts. Juris Doctor degree with expertise in advising lawyers on issues relating to professional responsibility. Prior teaching experience preferred. The salary range for this job is $2,000-$15,000 per semester, dependent on the number of credits offered, experience and practice area. We value diversity and strongly encourage individuals from a range of backgrounds to apply. Fordham Law School is a national leader in the field, with approximately 1,600 students in our J.D., LL.M. and M.S.L. programs. Fordham is the Jesuit University of New York and serves students of all faiths, including those with no faith. Interested candidates can apply at this link . For questions, please contact Audrey Glassman at aglassman@law.fordham.edu . Interested candidates can apply at this link ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/154638": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty: Criminal Procedure: Investigative, School of Law",
      "description": "Department: Fordham University: Office of the Provost: Law Location: Lincoln Center Campus Fordham Law School invites applications for an adjunct instructor position to teach a Law course in Criminal Procedure: Investigative.  The Law School is particularly interested in courses that combine both theory and current legal practice. Responsibilities: • Teach an engaging course at the Law School exploring legal issues in Investigations in Criminal Procedure, including in-depth analysis of case law, statutes, regulations, and practice-oriented documents • Develop a thirteen-week syllabus that covers essential topics within a Criminal Procedure: Investigative or domain. Foster an inclusive and collaborative learning environment conducive to critical thinking, discussion, and debate. • Shape a set of learning objectives that students will master by the end of the course. • Evaluate and grade student course performance in alignment with the appropriate grading curves, providing constructive feedback to foster students’ understanding of core concepts. We value diversity and strongly encourage individuals from a range of backgrounds to apply. Fordham Law School is a national leader in the field, with approximately 1,600 students in our J.D., LL.M. and M.S.L. programs. Fordham is the Jesuit University of New York and serves students of all faiths, including those with no faith. • Juris Doctor degree with a specialization in Criminal Law subject area. • Prior teaching experience preferred. The salary range for this job is $2,000-$15,000 per semester, dependent on the number of credits offered, experience, and practice area. Interested candidates should submit a cover letter, curriculum vitae, and a brief statement outlining their approach to teaching and the topic and goals of their proposed course(s). Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier account and can send all application materials, including confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge."
